/* Problem : Find Kth Bit in Nth Binary String
Platform:Leetcode
Difficulty:Medium
Link: https://leetcode.com/problems/find-kth-bit-in-nth-binary-string/description/

//Intuition

Instead of constructing the whole string (which grows exponentially), observe the structure:
For any Sn:
Sn = [Left Part] + [Middle '1'] + [Right Part]
Sn-1 1 reverse(invert(Sn-1))
Length of Sn = (2^n - 1)
The middle index is always:
mid = (length / 2) + 1
Now think carefully:
If k < mid → Answer lies in left part, same as Sn-1
If k == mid → Always '1'
If k > mid → It lies in the mirrored inverted part
For the right half:
Mirror index = length - k + 1
Then invert the result
So instead of building strings, we recursively shrink the problem size.
This is a classic example of:
Divide problem into symmetric subproblem + handle transformation.

//Approach
Base Case:
If n == 1 → return '0'
Compute:
length = (1 << n) - 1
mid = (length / 2) + 1
Three cases:
If k < mid → solve in left half → findKthBit(n-1, k)
If k == mid → return '1'
If k > mid
Mirror index = length - k + 1
Get bit from findKthBit(n-1, mirrorIndex)
Invert it



//Code
class Solution {
public:
    char findKthBit(int n, int k) {
        if(n==1)return '0';
        int length=(1<<n)-1;
        if(k<ceil(length/2.0))
        {
            return findKthBit(n-1,k);
        }
        else if(k==ceil(length/2.0))
        {
            return '1';
        }
        else
        {
            char ch=findKthBit(n-1,length-(k-1));
            return (ch=='0')?'1':'0';
        }
    }
};


//Time Complexity
At every recursive call:
n decreases by 1
Only one recursive branch is taken
So:
Time Complexity = O(n)
Since maximum depth = n


//Space Complexity
Recursion stack depth = n
Space Complexity = O(n)
No extra data structures used.
